## DESCRIPTION
## WeBWorK Demo
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by jjh2b

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Systems of linear equations)
## DBsection(Systems with 2 variables)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('lines')

# updated 2023.10.30

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", 'PGML.pl',
    'parserRadioMultiAnswer.pl',, 'contextFraction.pl', 'MatrixReduce.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric')->variables->add(y => 'Real');
Context()->noreduce('(-x)+y', '(-x)-y');

do {
    $A = Matrix([
        [ non_zero_random(-5, 5), non_zero_random(-5, 5) ],
        [ non_zero_random(-5, 5), non_zero_random(-5, 5) ]
    ]);
} until $A->det != 0;

$x = Vector(random(-4, 4), random(-4, 4));
$b = $A * $x;

@lines = map {
    Formula($A->element($_, 1) . 'x+' . $A->element($_, 2) . 'y')->reduce
} (1 .. 2);

Context('Point');
$xpt = Point($x);

$rma = RadioMultiAnswer(
    [
        ['There is no solution to the linear system'],
        ['There are an infinite number of solutions to the linear system.'],
        [
            'There is a single solution to the linear system in the form %s',
            $xpt
        ]
    ],
    2
);

BEGIN_PGML

Solve the follow linear system using the Gauss-Jordon method:

[```\begin{aligned}
[$lines[0]] & = [@ Matrix($b)->element(1,) @] \\
[$lines[1]] & = [@ Matrix($b)->element(2,) @] \\
\end{aligned}
```]
[_]{$rma}

If there is a single solution, write the answer like (3,4).
END_PGML

Context('Fraction');    # for fraction arithmetic

# augmented matrix to be reduced
$aug = Matrix([
    [ $A->element(1, 1), $A->element(1, 2), Matrix($b)->element(1) ],
    [ $A->element(2, 1), $A->element(2, 2), Matrix($b)->element(2) ]
]);

$aug_red = rref($aug);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

First, start with the augmented coefficient matrix or
[``` [$aug] ```]

and then perform Gauss-Jordon reduction to get 
[``` [$aug_red] ```]

The left 2 by 2 matrix is the identity matrix, so the solution is in the right column or
[``[$x]``]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
COMMENT('The solution to this homogeneous system is a single point.');

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
